# tools

Tool-side enforcement layer. Every model write passes through this
module; honesty guards and SpecDD Lever 2 Forbids enforcement live here.

## Must
- `write_file` and `edit_file` run honesty guards (role-path, placeholder, mass-deletion) before any I/O
- SpecDD Lever 2 `_check_spec_forbids` runs after honesty guards (cheaper guards first)
- `SddParseError` catch precedes `ValueError` catch in fs.py — `SddParseError` subclasses `ValueError`
- All tool errors return as `(str, str | None)` tuples; never raise to the caller
- `get_repo_root()` is the canonical accessor — never `from luxe.tools.fs import _REPO_ROOT`
- `cve_lookup` is gated to `task_type == "manage"` only (surface bloat regresses implement)
- New honesty-pattern guards land with a regression test in `tests/test_tools.py`

- The placeholder guard's prose exemption is CHAT-ONLY (2026-07-30):
  `make_prose_aware_write_fns()` skips `_check_placeholder_text` for prose
  extensions (`_PROSE_EXTENSIONS`: .txt/.md/.rst/.log/…) because in notes and
  session transcripts "# TODO: implement …" is user-dictated content, not a
  code stub. All other guards apply unchanged. Reachable ONLY via the chat
  front-end's write-mode extra-tool seam (same pattern as unrestricted bash);
  the module `TOOL_FNS`/`MUTATION_FNS` keep `prose_exempt=False`, so the
  benchmark/maintain path is byte-identical

- `bash` defaults to the hardened allowlist + chain/redirect rejection
  (`_validate_command`). The module `TOOL_FNS["bash"]` MUST stay restricted —
  every benchmark/maintain run uses it. The unrestricted variant
  (`make_bash_fn(unrestricted=True)` + `unrestricted_bash_def()`) is reachable
  ONLY via `luxe chat` dev mode (opt-in `/bash`, write-mode only), passed through
  `run_single`'s extra-tool seam; it never becomes the default fn

- `make_bash_fn` carries two further CHAT-ONLY seams (2026-07-31):
  `cancel` (duck-typed `.requested` — never an import from luxe.chat; tools
  must not depend on the chat layer) switches `_bash` to `_run_cancellable`,
  a Popen runner that polls the token (~0.2s) and SIGKILLs the process GROUP
  (start_new_session) so esc doesn't block on a hung command until the
  60/600s timeout; `on_start` fires with the command string BEFORE execution
  (dispatch-time visibility) and is exception-guarded — a broken UI callback
  never fails the tool. `cancel=None` (the bench default, and the only value
  `TOOL_FNS["bash"]` can produce) keeps the original `subprocess.run` path
  byte-identically; the timeout is enforced on both paths

- `read_file`'s size gate applies to UNWINDOWED reads only (2026-08-11). A
  call that passes `limit` is served at any file size — `_MAX_FILE_SIZE`
  (256 KB) bounds what may be read WHOLE, `_MAX_READ_BYTES` bounds what any
  single call RETURNS, and an output clipped by the latter carries the
  `offset=` to resume from. The gate used to run before `offset`/`limit` were
  read, which made the tool's own "use offset/limit for large files"
  description false and dead-ended the model on a 442 KB log (session
  0e524f033300; see `tests/test_read_file_large.py` and lessons.md
  2026-08-11). Two consequences are load-bearing: the refusal message MUST
  name the windowed call to make next rather than only the limit it hit, and
  the binary sniff MUST read 8 KB rather than the whole file then slice —
  windows are now accepted at sizes where that would load the file to decide
  it was binary. The tool DESCRIPTION is unchanged and golden-pinned
  (`tests/test_golden_request.py`); this is an error-path + capability fix, so
  the benchmark request body stays byte-identical

- `_grep` MUST pass `stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL` (2026-08-12). Invoked with no
  path argument, ripgrep chooses between "search cwd" and "read stdin" by
  inspecting stdin, and an inherited OPEN PIPE sends it down the stdin branch
  → EOF → exit 1 → this function reported `(no matches)`. Silently, for every
  search, in every NON-INTERACTIVE luxe: benchmarks launched from a script,
  CI, and the headless chat form the README documents. A TTY session was
  unaffected, which is why it survived — the failure is invisible in exactly
  the configuration a human watches. Do NOT "fix" it by appending a `.` search
  path: that prefixes every result with `./`, changing the output format the
  benchmark and the golden request see. Pinned by
  `tests/test_grep_silent_failures.py`

- A tool's FAILURE is never reported as a RESULT (2026-08-12). The pattern
  behind four fixes on the same day, all of the same shape — a command failed,
  the failure produced empty output, and the empty output was rendered as an
  ordinary answer:
  * `_grep` mapped every rg exit code through `_grep_result(stdout)`, so an
    error (exit ≥ 2: an unclosed group, an unreadable path) read as
    "(no matches)". Exit 1 is no-matches; exit ≥ 2 returns `("", stderr)`.
  * `_grep` also passes the pattern behind `--`, or a pattern that starts with
    a dash is consumed as a FLAG (`grep("-i")` found nothing, case-insensitively).
    rg treats `-- PATTERN` and `PATTERN` identically, so results don't move.
  * `_grep`'s `TimeoutExpired` is caught — a tool returns `(str, str | None)`
    and never raises (see Must above); only `FileNotFoundError` was.
  * `analysis._run_tool` never consulted `returncode`, so a crashed linter
    returned `{"status":"ok","findings":[],"count":0}` — the "lint passed"
    misread `_skipped` exists to prevent. `_crashed()` is its sibling: non-zero
    exit AND nothing parseable (a JSON tool that emitted no JSON, or no output
    at all) → `status:error` with the exit code and stderr. A non-zero exit
    WITH findings stays `ok` — that is what mypy does when it works.
  * `tools/git.py` model-supplied refs go behind `--end-of-options` and
    `git_log`'s `n` through `int()`: `git_diff(ref="--output=/tmp/x.patch")`
    made a READ-ONLY tool write a file outside the repo and returned
    `("", None)`. Ordinary refs/logs/shows are byte-identical (pinned by
    `tests/test_git_tool_truncation.py`)

- No tool spawn inherits luxe's stdin (2026-08-12). `_grep` was fixed first
  (below); the rule generalised the same day to `shell._bash` and
  `shell._run_cancellable`, `analysis._run_tool`, `pr._run`,
  `spec_validator._eval_tests_pass`, `gitkit.apply._run_verify` and
  `mcp_pdf.ops`. A child that reads stdin reads LUXE's: with a piped parent
  (script-launched bench, CI, the headless `printf … | luxe chat` form)
  `bash("cat")` returned the session's own queued input as tool output AND
  drained it; under a TTY it blocks for the whole timeout. Every one of those
  spawns also decodes with `errors="replace"` — one non-UTF-8 byte used to
  raise `UnicodeDecodeError` and discard the entire captured output
  (`luxe.fswalk` set the precedent)

- Tool truncation is ANNOUNCED, never silent (2026-08-12). `_grep_result`
  reports both of grep's caps — ripgrep's per-file `--max-count=150` and the
  32 KB byte slice — because a model asked to count something with 1,286
  matches was handed exactly 150 lines and nothing to suggest they were
  partial: a wrong answer wearing the shape of a complete one. The per-file
  count is taken over the FULL stdout, not the byte-truncated body, or a byte
  cut can hide the match cap behind it. Same rule as `read_file`'s window
  note: state the cut AND the way around it. A result under both caps is
  byte-identical to before

- Bash's 8 KB cap keeps the HEAD and the TAIL (`_clip_output`, 2026-08-12),
  not the first 8 KB: test runners put the failure summary at the END, so
  the old form reliably discarded exactly the lines the model needed
  (pytest's short summary, exit-status context). 2 KB head + 6 KB tail
  around an announced omission marker, snapped to line boundaries when they
  exist within the kept budgets; under the cap byte-identical. The shape
  could not be A/B'd on maintain_suite — 0 of 183 bash calls across six
  full 2026-08-12 suite passes reach the cap (the 205 historical firings
  are SWE-bench-era) — so the evidence is `tests/test_bash_truncation.py`
  plus the same-day 3-rep confirmation run
  (`acceptance/bash_tail_trunc_2026_08_12/`); SWE-bench is the venue where
  the shape change could show score effects, if anyone re-benches there

- Listings announce oversized files (`_oversize_note`, 2026-08-12). `list_dir`
  and `glob` annotate any entry `read_file` would refuse, so the model learns
  the limit from the listing instead of from a failed call — bare names meant
  one round-trip per discovery. Annotates ONLY files past the limit, so a tree
  with nothing oversized produces byte-identical output

- `read_file`'s limits are overridable per process via `set_read_budget()` /
  `budget_for_ctx()` (2026-08-12), OFF by default (`None` = the fixed 256 KB
  constants, which the benchmark/maintain path always gets). The fixed cap
  predates the `/ctx` tiers: in real tokens one max-size read is 480% of the
  DEFAULT 32K window and still 60% of the LARGEST window luxe can open, so
  scaling it with ctx means scaling it DOWN. This module keeps NO default of
  its own — the defaults live at the call sites below, and enabling it is a
  benchmark-path behaviour change (at num_ctx=32768 the whole-file limit
  becomes ~13 KB), which is why it took a maintain_suite A/B to promote

### `LUXE_TOOL_BUDGET_CTX` wiring (maintain DEFAULT-ON 2026-08-12; chat DEFAULT-ON 2026-08-24)

- **Both paths are default ON, each on its OWN dated evidence, and they share
  one grammar.** `maintain.apply_ctx_read_budget` (2026-08-12) and
  `chat/repl.py` (2026-08-24) each read
  `os.environ.get("LUXE_TOOL_BUDGET_CTX", "1")` and only the EXACT string
  `"0"` disables (unset → on; "", "true", "01", " 1" → on). That is the same
  opt-out grammar as `LUXE_TRUNCATED_TURN_RETRY` in `agents/flags.py`, and all
  three must stay spelled the same way. The paths agreeing is a RESULT, not a
  refactor: each default rests on its own arm, and neither may be moved on the
  other's evidence
- Chat's flip, benched 2026-08-24:
  `acceptance/chat_bigread_2026_08_24/REPORT.md` (planted repo — 250,040 B
  markdown + 70,028 B source — m1/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-4bit, `--backend local`,
  2 arms × 2 windows). OFF **hung unrecoverably** at 32768 (peak context
  pressure 1064.2%) and at 131072 (266.0%): the drill's own timeout killed the
  process group both times. ON completed both windows (60.0s / 79.9s, peak
  50.6% / 39.0%, 2 refused reads per arm) and the model **used the `offset=`
  resume** the clipped read hands it (1 / 2 calls) — 3 extra tool calls in
  total, which is the answer to the standing worry that a budget converts one
  fatal turn into three timid ones. Same shape in two real 2026-08-24
  incidents (sessions `168f1825a1fd`, `eb0b2923a3eb`) with `refused_reads=0`.
  Before this, chat was opt-in precisely because no chat-side arm existed;
  producing one is what made the alignment legal
- Maintain's flip, benched 2026-08-12:
  `acceptance/toolbudget_ab_2026_08_12/REPORT.md`
  (3 reps × 10 fixtures × 2 arms; both arms 30/30 · 120/150; tokens −3.9%,
  wall +4.3%; zero fixture regressions; opportunity exercised — 21 baseline
  over-budget reads vs zero in treatment; `read_budget_applied` in 30/30
  treatment runs and 0/30 baseline). The shipped default must stay
  byte-equivalent to that treatment arm: same `budget_for_ctx(num_ctx)` value,
  same console line, same event — the announce string still reads
  `LUXE_TOOL_BUDGET_CTX=1` for exactly that reason
- TWO call sites own the budget, one per path, and they never overlap:
  `chat/repl.py` sets it PER TURN (`/ctx` moves num_ctx mid-session, so the
  turn is the only correct scope), and `maintain.apply_ctx_read_budget` sets
  it ONCE per `maintain_pipeline`, from `cfg.role("monolith").num_ctx` — the
  same role config the run executes with, never a hardcoded window
- **BFCL is still UNWIRED and the flag is inert there**: its adapter calls
  `run_agent` in-process and never passes through `maintain_pipeline`. Wire a
  call site and preflight it before ever A/B-ing this flag on BFCL
- The bench wiring exists because the flag was previously **inert by
  construction on the benchmark path**: `set_read_budget`'s only caller was
  the chat front-end, so `LUXE_TOOL_BUDGET_CTX=1 python -m
  benchmarks.maintain_suite.run …` measured a no-op knob. This is the C10
  trap in `lessons.md` 2026-08-11 — a switch benched before it reached its
  target. Do not A/B this flag on any path without a call site on it
- **Off = no call at all**, not `set_read_budget(None)`. The reset is a
  documented no-op, but "don't touch the module state" is the stronger
  guarantee: with `=0`, `maintain_pipeline` runs byte-identically to before
  the wiring existed, and nothing can be re-ordered into a regression later.
  `read_limit()` stays `_MAX_FILE_SIZE`. That rule is the BENCH path's:
  `chat/repl.py`'s off branch deliberately does call `set_read_budget(None)`,
  because a chat turn must land on a known limit rather than inherit whatever
  the process last set
- The two paths still cannot reach each other. `maintain_pipeline` is not on
  the chat path (chat enters via `chat/launch.py` → `_run_interactive`), and
  even in a hypothetical shared process `repl.py` re-sets the budget on EVERY
  turn in BOTH branches, so its value is the one in force when that turn's
  tools run
- When ON the run says so — a `read_budget_applied` event in `events.jsonl`
  (`max_bytes`, `num_ctx`) plus a console line — so a bench arm can be
  checked for liveness instead of assumed live. Both are emitted ONLY when
  the switch is on, keeping the `=0` arm's console output and event stream
  unchanged. Pinned by `tests/test_tool_budget.py`

- `make_write_gated_fns()` (2026-08-11) registers CHAT-ONLY stubs for the
  tools `make_read_only_role` strips, as FNS with NO matching DEFS: the model
  is still not offered them (`/write` remains the gate) but a hallucinated
  call gets "…is DISABLED … gated, not missing … tell the user to run /write"
  instead of `Unknown tool: edit_file`, which is false and ended a turn with a
  composed file body discarded (session 0e524f033300 run -14). Keep the key
  set equal to `mcp.server._MUTATION_TOOL_NAMES` — a tool stripped without a
  stub silently regains the wrong message — and keep genuinely unknown names
  producing the "Unknown tool" prefix, which `loop.py`'s `tool_reject`
  telemetry keys on. Same precedent as `make_bash_fn(restricted_hint=True)`:
  the front-end owns the wording of its own toggles

- `_CHAT_EXTRA_ALLOW` (2026-08-05, currently `{gh}`) widens the allowlist for
  CHAT bash only: applied inside `make_bash_fn` (both restricted and
  unrestricted modes route through it; a no-op in the latter) and advertised
  by `restricted_bash_def()`. The module defaults — `TOOL_FNS["bash"]`,
  `tool_defs()`, and the rejection message with `extra_allow` empty — stay
  byte-identical: a benchmark that can reach GitHub's API is not
  reproducible, and the default description is golden-pinned. Remote
  mutations via gh ride GIT_WORKFLOW_HINT's explicit-ask rule

## Must not
- Bypass `_safe()` path scoping in any new tool
- Hardcode tool-allowlist subsets in `single.py` (the role config is the single source of truth)
- Wire unrestricted bash into the benchmark/maintain path or the default
  `TOOL_FNS` — it is a chat-front-end carve-out only

## Owns
- src/luxe/tools/**
